   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to requiring the commissioner of health to conduct a study\nrelating to the number of emergency room beds",
    "basePrintNo": "S4699",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4699-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Health, Commissioner of",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4699\n\nSPONSOR: RAMOS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act in relation to requiring the commissioner of\nhealth to conduct a study relating to the number of emergency room beds\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo determine and establish the number of emergency beds required per\ncapita, the maximum distance patients shall be expected to travel to\naccess emergency services, and other such relevant information.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill directs the commissioner of health to study the\nnumber of emergency room beds in the state and establish the minimum\nnumber of beds required. The study will also assess the distance\npatients need to travel to access emergency room services. From these\nfindings, the commissioner shall make recommendations ranging from the\nnumber of emergency room beds required per capita to the maximum\ndistance patients shall be expected to travel to access emergency\nservices. A report based on this study shall be submitted to the gover-\nnor, the temporary president of the senate, the speaker of the assembly\nand chairs of the senate and assembly standing committees on health no\nlater than December 31, 2019.\n\nSection 2 relates to the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccessing quality health care should not be a burden. Yet, many New\nYorkers faced with a medical emergency may find it difficult to receive\nadequate care. Overcrowded emergency departments resulting from too few\nbeds may cause patients to wait far longer than what is appropriate.\nAlso, a hospital's proximity can be an issue for patients who must trav-\nel great lengths to access those services. In situations as serious and\nas time-sensitive as a medical emergency, distance and wait times should\nnot be of concern. The availability of emergency room beds are bound to\nworsen as New York's population ages and hospitals continue to close.\nAside from keeping us better informed, a report by the commissioner of\nhealth would serve as a first step to addressing this pressing health\nissue.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
